# NOTE4C Control Tower — environment
#
# Copy to .env.local and set what applies to your machine. Every variable here
# is OPTIONAL, and every one of them defaults to "not configured" rather than
# to somebody else's value. A tower with this file untouched runs against the
# in-repo mock, reports each unconfigured source as "not configured" instead of
# broken, and makes no outbound request at all.
#
# .env.local is gitignored. Nothing in this file is a secret — these are paths,
# origins and coordinates. Actual credentials live in the tower's own 0700
# secrets directory, or in a file you point one of these variables at.


# ── Where the tower keeps its data ───────────────────────────────────────────

# The private data root: state, dashboards, versions, ledger, audit, secrets.
# Created at mode 0700. Default: ~/.note4c-control-tower
# NOTE4C_TOWER_DATA_DIR=/Users/you/.note4c-control-tower


# ── The device ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The address a fresh tower starts with. Must be a private IPv4 literal
# (10/8, 172.16/12 or 192.168/16); hostnames are refused by design. Leave it
# unset and the tower asks for one in the interface, which is the normal way.
# NOTE4C_DEVICE_ADDRESS=<panel-ip>

# A file holding an existing device token, if you already have one from another
# program. When set, the Device page offers to copy it into the tower's own
# secrets directory at 0600, naming this exact file in the consent dialog. The
# value is never displayed, logged, or returned by any route. Unset by default,
# and the button does not appear at all.
# NOTE4C_BRIDGE_TOKEN_PATH=/path/to/device-token

# ── What the tower binds ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `npm start` and `npm run dev` go through tools/tower-serve.ts, which applies
# the bind policy BEFORE the server exists. A host it refuses means no socket is
# opened at all, and the command exits non-zero.

# The address to listen on. Default: localhost. Anything outside the loopback range
# needs NOTE4C_TOWER_ALLOW_LAN=1 and a configured passphrase, both; binding to
# every interface is refused outright even with both, because that includes
# ones you did not mean. Name the single address instead.
# NOTE4C_TOWER_HOST=<lan-ip>

# The port. Default: 8654.
# NOTE4C_TOWER_PORT=8654

# Permit a non-loopback bind. Requires a passphrase to be set as well, and
# binding every interface is still refused. Think before using this: the tower
# holds a credential that can repaint your wall, and anything that can reach
# the address above can reach the login page.
# NOTE4C_TOWER_ALLOW_LAN=1


# ── The panel's clock ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# An IANA timezone name for the dates and times drawn on the panel. Defaults to
# this machine's own zone, which is usually right. A name this runtime cannot
# resolve is ignored rather than allowed to fail every render.
#
# Note: the browser preview resolves its default the same way, so setting this
# for the server process alone can make the preview and the panel differ by an
# offset. Set it for both, or leave it unset.
# NOTE4C_PANEL_TIMEZONE=Etc/UTC


# ── Weather (optional source) ────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where the forecast is for. Both halves or neither: a latitude with no
# longitude is not a location, and the tower will not guess the other half.
# Unset, the weather module renders "not configured" and nothing is fetched.
#
# Use a COARSE location — a city centre, not your address. The panel prints the
# label beside the numbers as provenance, a coordinate pair in a config file
# ends up in your backups, and a forecast is the same across a city anyway.
# NOTE4C_WEATHER_LATITUDE=51.51
# NOTE4C_WEATHER_LONGITUDE=-0.13

# What the panel prints as the source of those numbers. Defaults to the
# coordinates themselves, rounded — honest, if ugly.
# NOTE4C_WEATHER_LABEL=City centre approx.

# An optional second forecast source, used only when the first one fails. The
# parser expects the shape published by Environment and Climate Change Canada's
# city page feed, which is why this is a whole URL rather than a place: there is
# no general way to derive a national service's per-city identifier from a
# latitude. If you set this, that service's terms of use are yours to honour.
# NOTE4C_WEATHER_FALLBACK_URL=https://api.weather.gc.ca/collections/citypageweather-realtime/items/<province>-<id>?f=json


# ── Calendar (optional source, macOS) ────────────────────────────────────────

# A JSON snapshot written by an EventKit worker. The tower READS this file and
# never writes to that directory, never starts or stops the worker, and never
# touches its runtime state. The worker itself is not part of this repository.
# NOTE4C_CALENDAR_SNAPSHOT_PATH=/path/to/.calendar-private/snapshot.json

# Optionally assert which calendar the snapshot must be of. Set it and a
# snapshot of a different calendar is refused rather than rendered; leave it
# unset and whatever the worker exported is accepted.
# NOTE4C_CALENDAR_NAME=Shared


# ── Apple Reminders (optional source, macOS) ─────────────────────────────────

# Absolute path to the `remindctl` binary. No PATH lookup is performed: the
# tower spawns this without a shell, and resolving a bare name through the
# environment is how the wrong binary gets run. Unset, the Reminders source is
# not configured and nothing is spawned.
#
# The tower is read-only toward Reminders: only `list`, `show` and `status` may
# be run, only the COUNT of open and overdue items ever leaves the Mac, and a
# reminder's notes are dropped at the parse boundary.
# NOTE4C_REMINDCTL_BIN=/opt/homebrew/bin/remindctl


# ── Home Assistant sensors (optional source) ─────────────────────────────────

# Either set HASS_URL and HASS_TOKEN in the environment directly, or point this
# at a dotenv file that holds them. The process environment wins. Credentials
# are read per request and never copied into the tower's own store; the token
# never crosses back over the HTTP boundary.
# NOTE4C_HA_ENV_PATH=/path/to/.env
# HASS_URL=http://homeassistant.local:8123
# HASS_TOKEN=...

# Only sensor.* and binary_sensor.* entities can ever be displayed.


# ── A second renderer, if you happen to run one (optional) ───────────────────

# Origin of another program the tower may READ the status of, and never writes
# to. Very specific to the installation this project grew out of. Unset, the
# card disappears from Overview and Diagnostics entirely rather than reporting
# "not running" forever about a program you do not have.
# NOTE4C_COMPOSER_ORIGIN=http://localhost:9731


# ── Testing and development ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

# How long the mock device pretends a panel refresh takes, in milliseconds.
# The real panel takes tens of seconds; the browser suite uses a short one.
# NOTE4C_MOCK_PANEL_MS=700

# How long a push waits for the device to confirm it displayed the frame.
# NOTE4C_PUSH_MAX_WAIT_MS=12000

# Unlocks the test-only mock control route and swaps live sources for fixtures,
# so the browser suite reaches nothing outside this repository. Only the
# Playwright web server sets this. Do not set it for a real tower.
# NOTE4C_TOWER_E2E=1

# Starts the background refresh scheduler. Off unless explicitly enabled, so a
# build, a test or a development server never opens the real push pipeline.
# Intended for a LaunchAgent or a systemd unit, not for a terminal.
# NOTE4C_TOWER_SCHEDULER=1

# Maintainer-only: a second implementation of pack() to cross-check against.
# See tools/parity_check.py.
# NOTE4C_REFERENCE_RENDERER=/path/to/other/renderer.py

# Maintainer-only: where tools/gen_font_atlas.py writes the glyph atlases.
# Unset, it writes straight into src/core/render/fonts/, which is where the
# renderer reads them from. Point it at a scratch directory to CHECK that the
# generator is deterministic — regenerate, then diff against the committed
# files — instead of taking that claim on trust.
# NOTE4C_ATLAS_OUT=/tmp/note4c-atlases
